The problem stems from a regular function passed to `setInterval` receiving its own `this` value. When not in strict mode, `this` refers to the global object (`window` or `globalThis`), which lacks a `seconds` property. Consequently, `this.seconds += 1` evaluates to `undefined + 1`, producing `NaN`, which then overwrites the value and gets printed to the console.

**Solution:** Replace the regular function with an arrow function, which preserves the `this` context from the enclosing `start()` method (the `Timer` instance):

```javascript
class Timer {
  constructor() {
    this.seconds = 0;
  }
  start() {
    setInterval(() => {
      this.seconds += 1;
      console.log(this.seconds);
    }, 1000);
  }
}
new Timer().start();
```

If a regular function must be used, you can resolve this by either explicitly binding it to the current context (`setInterval(function () {...}.bind(this), 1000)`) or saving `this` to a variable before the callback (`const self = this;`, then reference `self.seconds` within the function).

